  "seed_canonical_sha256": "1ccaa407367f5286d79764ee6ccd12d190e19455c80db228064a58efd967cd97",
  "read_order": "Only seed prose and skill/review/contract/production-resume plus engineering/operations and checker source read. No candidate views, draft, keys, author intent, answers, old choices, interactions, history, ticket outputs or images read. No DB/git/browser/model/install/promotion operations.",
  "domain": "All variables real unless the source explicitly asks for integer solutions. Reciprocal denominators nonzero. Source 154 imposes no sign restriction on r; the radius-only interpretation is separately noted.",
  "solutions": [
    {"source_number": "138", "independent_solution": "Substitution in x^2-3xy+y^2 gives a=31, b=31, c=31, d=31, e=-5, f=31. For f, x+y=6, xy=1, x^2+y^2=34, so 34-3=31. Exactly a,b,c,d,f are solutions."},
    {"source_number": "139", "independent_solution": "Over R: a has x=+/-sqrt(7), y arbitrary, infinitely many. b has x=2y, since x^2+1>0, infinitely many. c is the union x=+/-3 or y=+/-2, infinitely many. d has exactly the four pairs (3,2),(3,-2),(-3,2),(-3,-2), since both squared terms vanish. e is x=y; the zero of x^2+y^2 is the origin already on that line, infinitely many. f forces x=y=1, exactly one."},
    {"source_number": "140", "independent_solution": "The left sides equal a:(x-y)^2+8>=8; b:(x+4)^2+y^2+1>=1; c:(x-5)^2+y^2+75>=75; d:(x-y)^2+(y+2)^2+3>=3. Each bound is strictly positive, so each equation has no real solution."},
    {"source_number": "141", "independent_solution": "a becomes (x+3)^2+(y-5)^2=0, giving only (-3,5). b becomes (x-3y)^2+(x-5)^2=0, giving x=5 and y=5/3. Both sums of nonnegative squares vanish only when every square does, proving existence and uniqueness."},
    {"source_number": "142", "independent_solution": "Examples with exact finite real solution sets: no solutions: x^2+y^2+1=0; one: x^2+y^2=0, only (0,0); two: x^2(x-1)^2+y^2=0, only (0,0),(1,0); three: x^2(x-1)^2(x-2)^2+y^2=0, only (0,0),(1,0),(2,0). Nonnegative summands must each vanish. These are examples, not a classification of all possible equations."},
    {"source_number": "143", "independent_solution": "a none: 3(x-y) is divisible by 3, not 4. b yes: (1,13),(13,1),(-1,-13),(-13,-1), the full integer set. c none: an integer product cannot be -7.5. d y!=0 and (x-1)y^2=7; the only squared integer divisors of 7 are 1, giving (8,1),(8,-1). e x,y!=0 and (x-6)(y-6)=36: (x,y)=(6+d,6+36/d) for signed integer divisors d of 36 except d=-6 (which gives the excluded (0,0)). Full 17 pairs: (7,42),(8,24),(9,18),(10,15),(12,12),(15,10),(18,9),(24,8),(42,7),(5,-30),(4,-12),(3,-6),(2,-3),(-3,2),(-6,3),(-12,4),(-30,5). f (x-y)(x+y)=11 with odd signed factor pairs, giving (6,5),(6,-5),(-6,5),(-6,-5). Existence exactly b,d,e,f."},
    {"source_number": "144", "independent_solution": "a at (5,-4), x^3+2y^2+3=125+32+3=160!=0, so A is not on the graph. b at (7,-3), 49+1029+2079+70+57+24=3308!=0, so B is not on its graph. The unusual x^2y and xy^3 terms are kept exactly as seeded."},
    {"source_number": "145", "independent_solution": "a union of entire lines x=4 and y=3, crossing (4,3). b union of entire lines x=1 and y=x, crossing (1,1). c union of xy=6 (two hyperbola branches in quadrants I,III, x!=0) and y=-x (entire line); no mutual intersections because -x^2=6 is impossible. d factors (x-y)(x+y)=0: union of entire lines y=x and y=-x, crossing the origin."},
    {"source_number": "146", "independent_solution": "a y=4-|x|: inverted V, vertex (0,4), y=4+x for x<=0 and y=4-x for x>=0, x-intercepts +/-4. b x=4-|y|: left-opening V, vertex (4,0), x=4-y for y>=0 and x=4+y for y<=0, x<=4, y-intercepts +/-4. c diamond boundary with vertices (4,0),(0,4),(-4,0),(0,-4); four closed segments with the appropriate signed linear forms. d (|x|-x)+(|y|-y)=0: filled closed first quadrant x>=0,y>=0, including both positive axes and origin, not just a boundary. e y=6/|x|, x!=0: positive upper branches in I,II, y=6/x for x>0, y=-6/x for x<0; symmetry in y-axis and no axis points. f xy=6 or xy=-6, four branches in all four quadrants, x,y!=0; symmetric in both axes. Hyperbolic branches have coordinate-axis asymptotes."},
    {"source_number": "147", "independent_solution": "a y=-5/x, x!=0: rectangular hyperbola in II,IV with coordinate-axis asymptotes; points (-1,5),(1,-5),(-5,1),(5,-1). b y=7.5/x, x!=0: rectangular hyperbola in I,III with the same asymptotes; points (1,7.5),(3,2.5),(-1,-7.5),(-3,-2.5). Neither curve meets an axis."},
    {"source_number": "148", "independent_solution": "Each is the full circle centered at (0,0), with radii respectively 7, sqrt(48)=4sqrt(3), sqrt(42.25)=6.5, sqrt(20.25)=4.5. Axis extrema are (+/-radius,0),(0,+/-radius). Radius is the nonnegative square root, not the right-side number or its square."},
    {"source_number": "149", "independent_solution": "From a^2+b^2=r^2, every listed point B,C,D,E,K,M has the identical sum of coordinate squares a^2+b^2. All six are on the same circle, for all admissible a,b,r; sign reversal and coordinate exchange preserve the sum. Zero coordinates do not change this conclusion."},
    {"source_number": "150", "independent_solution": "A gives r^2=8^2+15^2=289, r=17. B: 7^2+16^2=305>289, outside. C: 9^2+14^2=277<289, inside. D: (-1)^2+(12sqrt(2))^2=1+288=289, on the circumference."},
    {"source_number": "151", "independent_solution": "Graphical intersections can be derived exactly to verify every branch. a y=x-1 gives x^2-x+12=0, discriminant -47: empty. b y=8-x gives 2x^2-16x-17=0: (4+7sqrt(2)/2,4-7sqrt(2)/2) and the coordinate swap. c y=11-2x gives 5x^2-44x+96=0: (4,3),(24/5,7/5). d y=x^2 gives 2x^2+3x-3=0: x=(-3+/-sqrt(33))/4, y=(21-/+3sqrt(33))/8 with correlated signs. e y=x-10 and y=-x-x^2 give x^2+2x-10=0: (-1+sqrt(11),-11+sqrt(11)),(-1-sqrt(11),-11-sqrt(11)). f s=x+y=+/-4, d=x-y=+/-2 independently: (3,1),(1,3),(-3,-1),(-1,-3). Every listed pair satisfies both equations. Graph-production and graphical location are separate source methods."},
    {"source_number": "152", "independent_solution": "Substitution: a y=2x-1, 5x=8: (8/5,11/5). b x=5+4y, 12y=-12: (1,-1). c x=20-2y, y^2-10y+24=0: (12,4),(8,6). d y=(3x-6)/2, x^2-2x-80=0: (10,12),(-8,-15). e y=2x+1, 2x^2-8=0: (2,5),(-2,-3). f x=-2-2y, 2y^2-3y-27=0: (-11,9/2),(4,-3). Quadratic branches are complete; none was removed by division."},
    {"source_number": "153", "independent_solution": "a set s=x+y, s^2=3 and s+2y=-1: ((1+3sqrt(3))/2,(-1-sqrt(3))/2),((1-3sqrt(3))/2,(-1+sqrt(3))/2). b y=2x-1 gives -2x^2+6x=0: (0,-1),(3,5). c 2x=11-y gives y^2-4y-5=0: (3,5),(6,-1). d x=y+4 gives y^2-9y+14=0: (11,7),(6,2). e (2x-3y)(2x+3y)=5(2x+3y), then 11x-25y-19=0 with 2x-3y=5: only (4,1). f x=y-2 gives 4y^2+9y-9=0: (-5/4,3/4),(-5,-3). Original equations verify every retained pair."},
    {"source_number": "154", "independent_solution": "x+y=6 implies x^2+y^2>=18, equality only (3,3). No real solution iff r^2<18, or -3sqrt(2)<r<3sqrt(2), for unrestricted real r. If r is explicitly a nonnegative radius, this intersects to 0<=r<3sqrt(2). Equality at r=+/-3sqrt(2) has one point (3,3); r^2>18 has two: (3+sqrt(r^2/2-9),3-sqrt(r^2/2-9)) and its swap. Endpoints must not be included in the no-solution interval."},
    {"source_number": "155", "independent_solution": "With x+y=c and x^2+y^2=100, (x-y)^2=200-c^2>=0. Solutions exist iff -10sqrt(2)<=c<=10sqrt(2). Inside there are two swapped pairs ((c+sqrt(200-c^2))/2,(c-sqrt(200-c^2))/2); at either endpoint there is exactly one, x=y=c/2. Outside, none."},
    {"source_number": "156", "independent_solution": "Every subtask requires x!=0,y!=0. a 6(x+y)=xy, y=2x-5 gives 2x^2-23x+30=0: (10,15),(3/2,-2). b 20(y-x)=xy, x=14-2y gives y^2+23y-140=0: (4,5),(70,-28). c mixed number is 25/12; 12(x^2+y^2)=25xy and sum 14 give xy=48: (6,8),(8,6). d 6(x^2-y^2)=5xy and x-y=2 give 5x^2-34x+24=0: (6,4),(4/5,-6/5). All denominators are nonzero and substitution verifies all roots; no positive-only restriction is justified."},
    {"source_number": "157", "independent_solution": "a x=y or x=-y combined with 2x-y=1 gives (1,1),(1/3,-1/3). b x=3 or y=5: (3,4),(3,-4),(0,5); both product branches are essential. c x=7y or x=-7y gives y=+/-sqrt(2), four pairs (7sqrt(2),sqrt(2)),(-7sqrt(2),sqrt(2)),(7sqrt(2),-sqrt(2)),(-7sqrt(2),-sqrt(2)). d x=0 produces -y^2=50 and no real point; y=-1 gives (+/-sqrt(51),-1), exactly two."},
    {"source_number": "158", "independent_solution": "Use s=x+y,d=x-y with x=(s+d)/2,y=(s-d)/2. a s^2=16,d^2=64: (6,-2),(-2,6),(2,-6),(-6,2). b s^2=128,d^2=32: (6sqrt(2),2sqrt(2)),(2sqrt(2),6sqrt(2)),(-6sqrt(2),-2sqrt(2)),(-2sqrt(2),-6sqrt(2)). c s^2=27,d^2=3: (2sqrt(3),sqrt(3)),(sqrt(3),2sqrt(3)),(-2sqrt(3),-sqrt(3)),(-sqrt(3),-2sqrt(3)). Each uses both independent sign branches. d x^2+y^2=33 and xy=-24 would require s^2=-15, impossible; equivalently 33<2|xy|=48. Empty real set."},
    {"source_number": "159", "independent_solution": "The last two linear equations give x=3y-2, 2x-5y=-1, hence y=3,x=7. The first left side is 49-63+9+7+6=8, exactly its required value. Unique simultaneous solution (7,3). Pairwise satisfiability alone would not suffice."},
    {"source_number": "160", "independent_solution": "The first and third graphs imply 3x-2y=6 and x+y=7, giving only (4,3). At that point the second left side is 16+12-18-4+3=9, not 5. Therefore the three graphs have no common point. The first/third intersection exists but is not a simultaneous intersection of all three."}
  ]
}
